In plain terms: BIDeT reads what you give it, writes a picture where you say
(and overwrites a file named with -o without asking), and talks to your
terminal in escape sequences, including a good deal of SIXEL.  How a terminal
copes with that varies, and animation is the hardest part.  Some terminals,
Windows Terminal among them, leave noise or stale pixels behind; if one does,
type "reset", or write a GIF instead.  It is a hobby project written for an
audience of roughly one.

The obsolete versions are worse.  BIDeT 1 (v1/) depends on a patched netpbm and
a pile of other tools, and its fixnetpbm script, which removed system packages
and installed an unverified .deb as root, has been disabled.  Don't run
anything in v1/ or v2/ that you have not read first.

If it extrudes your letters through the floor, you get to keep both pieces.
